Subject: Carve Small Property Arrays Out Of The Object's Own Block

What this does
  For `JS_CLASS_OBJECT`, `JS_CLASS_BYTECODE_FUNCTION` and
  `JS_CLASS_FOR_IN_ITERATOR`, an object whose shape needs at most four property
  slots allocates its property array inside its own block -- `p->prop` points
  just past the object -- instead of in a second allocation. `JSObject` gains a
  `prop_inline_cap : 3` field recording how many slots live there.

Why we need it
  Every object was two allocations and two frees. An inline property array costs
  no second block header and no separate free, which is what the birth of an
  object literal, a closure and a for-in iterator pays for every instance.

What it adds
  One place that knows the layout: `js_prop_grow()`, which returns the inline
  array while the request still fits, and otherwise allocates a real block,
  copies the inline slots and clears the capacity. The four grow sites and the
  one free site route through it or through the capacity, and no read of
  `p->prop[i]` changes anywhere in the engine. Memory accounting counts the
  inline slots as property bytes and does not count a block for them.

  The inline area is sized to the shape, not to the maximum. MEASURED, same
  binary, 260,000 live objects: a fixed four-slot inline area costs +11.8% total
  heap against the two-block layout, while sizing it to the shape SAVES 3.4% and
  halves the allocation count either way (521,128 -> 281,102). The speed is the
  same in both, so the maximum is a footprint cost with no return.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No

